#
# Every source holding a string a user of this project reads, for the
# `evolution-jmap` gettext domain. `xgettext` reads this list and nothing else,
# so a marked string in an unlisted file is a string no translator is ever
# offered; `rust/crates/jmap-backend-core/tests/potfiles.rs` holds the list to
# the sources in both directions so that cannot happen quietly.
#
# Paths are relative to the directory holding this `po/`, which is gettext's
# convention and what the extraction has to be run from:
#
#   xgettext --files-from=po/POTFILES.in -L C --from-code=UTF-8 \
#            --keyword=N_ --keyword=translate --keyword=translate_with \
#            --add-comments=TRANSLATORS \
#            --package-name=evolution-jmap -o po/evolution-jmap.pot
#
# Every function in `jmap-backend-core`'s `i18n` that takes a msgid is a
# keyword here, `translate_with` included — its first argument is the message
# and its later ones are the values filled into the message's `%1$s`
# placeholders, which is the position `--keyword` defaults to.
#
# `-L C` because gettext 0.21 has no Rust parser. It extracts our markers
# correctly — `N_(c"…")` reads as a call with a string argument either way — but
# it lexes a lifetime as an opening character constant and says so:
#
#   provider.rs:125: warning: unterminated character constant
#
# Harmless where it stands today, and not something to leave unwatched: the
# lexer resumes at the next `'`, so a marked string between two lifetimes could
# in principle be swallowed. Whoever wires the extraction into the build should
# either check the `.pot` against this list or use a Rust-aware extractor.
#
# What is NOT here, and why:
#
# - `src/*.c`. The C example module is upstream skeleton code from the GNOME
#   Evolution wiki, and it carries its own gettext domain — the top-level
#   CMakeLists.txt still sets `GETTEXT_PACKAGE` to `example-module`, which is
#   not this project's domain. Its strings ("My Maildir Folder Action…") are
#   demonstration text that will leave with the skeleton; folding them into
#   this catalogue would hand translators strings for a module nobody ships.
# - Anything under a crate's `tests/`. A string that exists only in a test
#   binary is not one a user reads.
# - Protocol constants, JMAP property names, log and trace text, and errors
#   only a developer sees. The standing directive in `docs/ROADMAP.md` excludes
#   them deliberately: translating them makes bug reports unreadable and gains
#   no user anything.

rust/crates/jmap-backend-cal/src/ops.rs
rust/crates/jmap-mail/src/provider.rs
